WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.717 --> 00:00:04.220
If you're just getting into graphic
design, you are at a really fun point

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.388
in the learning process.

00:00:05.388 --> 00:00:07.073
It might seem intimidating at first,

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:10.643
but pretty much everybody these days
has full access to the resources,

00:00:10.794 --> 00:00:12.178
materials and tools

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:15.982
they need to do graphic design
for themselves and do it well.

00:00:16.232 --> 00:00:19.102
But not everybody knows about the few key
design principles

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:20.970
that we're going to go over in this video.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.089
So if you're looking
to elevate your artwork

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:27.210
above the rest and give it that final push
of professionalism and personality

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.097
it needs to really shine, let's
check out these five tips and tricks.

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.098
Let's get into it.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.717
If you don't already have

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:37.387
a favorite design software,
there are a ton to choose from out there.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.574
The link to our program Maestro
is in the description below: free, online,

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:45.979
and it gives you access to a whole lot
of templates, clipart, fonts and more.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.397
Okay, let's design.

00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:48.298
The first step might seem

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:51.501
a little bit obvious, but it's something
that's super important to nail down

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:52.368
early on.

00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:55.488
You have to tailor
your design for its intended purpose.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:59.459
So this could mean something as simple
as using red and green and snowflakes

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.461
if you're creating a design
for the holidays.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:05.765
It could also mean not using fonts
that are too intricate or hard to read

00:01:05.782 --> 00:01:08.935
if your design is going to be printed
onto something small, like a sticker.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:12.021
If you're designing for a business
or a brand, it's always important

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:15.775
to make sure that your design
stays in line with your brand image.

00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:19.229
A fun exercise to do
before you get into a design or a series

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:23.216
of designs is to create a mood board
with a lot of different pictures

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:25.769
and color palettes
that inspire your design.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:29.756
Tip number two is to include
visual hierarchy in your design.

00:01:29.973 --> 00:01:33.209
This means more than just differentiating
the size of your elements, too.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.247
This product label by Boomtown Roasters
Coffee is a really good

00:01:37.247 --> 00:01:40.316
example of implementing visual hierarchy
in a design.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.718
The image on the front of the label

00:01:41.718 --> 00:01:44.871
stands out because it's not in color
like the background behind it.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:48.241
And even though there's probably about
three different fonts being used here,

00:01:48.258 --> 00:01:51.811
the thickness of the font is
differentiated a lot and it helps you know

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:54.831
which pieces of text are important
and which are just details.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:59.869
Tip number three is knowing the importance
of negative space and using blank space

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:03.289
in your design instead of overcrowding it
with too many elements.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.743
A lot of times it's very hard
when you're designing to include

00:02:06.743 --> 00:02:10.046
all of the information
you want to include, especially

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:13.166
if you're doing something like a product
label or something with a lot of copy.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.502
But not everything can make it
to the final cut.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:19.739
Overcrowding your design can make it
confusing, hard for people to understand

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:23.493
or read, especially from a distance,
it just gets really complicated.

00:02:23.493 --> 00:02:27.780
So it's better to use a lot of white space
or negative space in your design

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.734
so that the elements that are included
stand out as much as they deserve to.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.453
This goat milk soap

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:37.457
from Kula Essentials is a great example
of utilizing negative space in a design.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.109
We have some elements on the side here
and some text in the center,

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:42.712
but a pretty good amount of this
label is just pure white.

00:02:43.346 --> 00:02:47.517
Tip number for it to be intentional
with the colors you use in your design.

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:51.054
We have a whole video on the color wheel,
color theory,

00:02:51.054 --> 00:02:54.357
and color psychology
that is linked in the description below.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.560
This one goes a little more in-depth
on the emotional effects

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.247
different colors can have and why
certain colors match and others don't.

00:03:01.798 --> 00:03:05.251
These custom stickers by Mugs
and Wildflowers are a great example

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.620
of choosing a limited color
palette for your design,

00:03:07.637 --> 00:03:09.622
even though there's
a pretty wide variety of colors

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:13.560
being shown here with blues, greens,
yellows and purples, they're all subdued

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.895
into this nice, pastel, earthy palette.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:19.499
It really helps the design look cohesive
and gives it a really nice aesthetic.

00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:22.735
Tip number five is to proofread.

00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:24.954
This is one I struggle with personally.

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:26.239
Let your design sit.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.258
This doesn't
just mean checking the grammar

00:03:28.258 --> 00:03:30.510
and making sure that all the copy
is grammatically correct.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.377
But this could also mean

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.363
just taking a little bit of time
for yourselves

00:03:33.363 --> 00:03:37.016
to get rid of the creative tunnel vision,
spend a few minutes doing something else

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.919
and then returning to your design
to make sure it looks okay.

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:43.339
It's just a good step to make sure
that all of your fonts are right,

00:03:43.339 --> 00:03:46.292
all of your elements are placed
exactly where you need them to be,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.345
and there's no other technical problems
that you'll need to worry about.

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:52.282
It's likely your design will go through
a few different drafts

00:03:52.282 --> 00:03:54.801
and naturally evolve
into its best version.

